pkg.description:
C3 application package this Type System element _belongs_ to; note that this maybe different from the package
where this type-system element was declared.

@see #package

name.description:
The name of the primitive type (such as `double` or `boolean`). This is usually the same as the DSL syntax,
except it does not include variations such as (`date` or `datetime with millis`) or modifiers (`!` and `?`).

@see #keyword

enumRefType.description:
This declares that the values are constrained to those defined on the named enum type. The fields of the enum
type must have the same type as the primitive type which is constrained by them.

```type
enum type Interval { SECOND MINUTE HOUR }

type X {
  interval: string enum Interval
}
```

@see Enum

title:
The DateTime value type has inner structure (fields):
 - precision: seconds / milliseconds / microseconds / nanoseconds
 - time zone: arbitrary / force UTC / no time zone
 - no time (logical date only)

Note that when parsing `datetime` values, excess precision beyond that declared in the value type will be discarded.
If sub-second precision is desired for a value, it must be declared that way.

Varions methods on **PrimitiveType** produce the most common variants:
 - {@link PrimitiveType#ofDateTime ofDateTime}
 - {@link PrimitiveType#ofDateOnly ofDateOnly}
 - {@link PrimitiveType#ofDateTimeMillis ofDateTimeMillis}
 - {@link PrimitiveType#ofDateTimeMicros ofDateTimeMicros}
 - {@link PrimitiveType#ofDateTimeNanos ofDateTimeNanos}
